er  from Carroll McKinstry who wrote the  book while the world watched a  Birmingham bombing survivor comes of age  during the civil rights movement from  book TVs coverage of the civil rights  movement and back live from the 16th  Street Baptist Church in Alabama Doug  Jones is joining us what was the bomb  made of and how was it placed just  adjacent to where you are that exploded  in the basement of that church well you  know Steve it's another one of those  unanswered questions the bomb was placed  underneath the bottom steps that led to  the back of the sanctuary there was a  door that was in the back of the  sanctuary and there were steps that led  down right across the large window that  led to the ladies lounge those steps  haven't been repaired they're not there  anymore it's a window and the steps have  were obliterated and have not been  repaired the bomb was placed right  underneath the bottom steps but one of  the great mysteries has always been what  was the bomb made of because the FBI  with all of their technicians they came  down they had boots on the ground within  minutes and certainly within hours  people from Washington DC were here at  collecting material but it was never  really determined all of the tests that  were made at the time could not  determine exactly what it was I think  that there is a very good chance it was  made of dynamite  12 to 14 sticks of dynamite dynamite was  very plentiful in Birmingham we had  several dynamite factories in Birmingham  it was not as controlled as it is today  so we believe it was dynamite but at the  end of the day we just could never prove  it one way or another we just know it  was a bomb and as you pointed out  Birmingham became bombing ham with more  than 50 bombs from the late 1940s  through the early 1960s did these  individuals responsible for the bombs  intend to kill  another great question you know for many  many years the theory behind the 16th  Street Baptist Church bombing case was  that it did not go off when it was  supposed to that no one was supposed to  get hurt there was some testimony about  that in the Chambliss trial there was a  little bit of testimony about that that  we had in the cherry trial where an  ex-wife and a brother-in-law of Cherry's  testified that he would on the one hand  say well the bomb didn't go off when it  was supposed to but at the same time he  would turn around and to show you the  kind of hatred and type of people we  were dealing with he would talk about  these beautiful little girls and say  well at least they can't breathe but at  the end of the day we didn't know one  way or another and I can always we were  fashioning our theory really not having  to determine one way or another whether  it went off when it was supposed to and  after Blanton and sherry were indicted  it was as you can imagine a great deal  of media here and they were released on  bond and when they got out on bond  awaiting trial there was a little bit of  disconcerning in the in the black  community here in Birmingham and there  was a lot of media of black leaders and  ministers who were complaining these  people had been off out free for  30-something years they should remain in  jail and so I called a meeting and I met  right here at the Civil Rights Institute  we were outside of today with a number  of the leaders because I was very  concerned that those kind of comments in  that media would cause a jury to be so  biased that we couldn't get a fair jury  and at that point I was asked a question  and I was asked a question by one of the  great leaders here reverend abraham  woods he asked me the same thing that i  believed that the bomb was meant to kill  people and i gave him the same history  lesson that i just said a minute ago and  he said well doc i'm really disappointed  to hear you say that because we believe  that with the march the children's  marches here in birmingham and the  desegregation orders that came about  with a school system because people do  forget that birmingham schools were  desegregated just days before this  bombing and with the march on washington  and the movement growing as it was the  Klan was unhappy and they were they were  just basically ignorant  people and the only way that they could  figure it out how to stop it was to do  more violence and to finally start  trying to do damage which kill people  and he reminded me of a bomb that took  place two weeks after this here on  Centre Street here in Birmingham and  there were actually two bombs one was a  decoy bomb that brought people out and  the second one was a shrapnel bomb  designed to kill people and so it became  our theory at that point that we  fashioned everything around the fact  that these children's marches put a  bull's-eye on the children they put a  bull's-eye on the church and the Klan  was unhappy and they decided that they  would kill people I don't know if it was  right or wrong  I dared some of the old Klansmen when we  talked to him to tell me I'm wrong  to tell me and you get on the stand tell  me what happened and he explained - am I  wrong not a single one of them ever did  so Steve that's my story and I'm  sticking to it  but there is an oath of silence among  members of the KKK can you explain well  there's an oath of silence which was it  was kind of interesting because if  anybody ever has ever gone through the  Klan files in the FBI you will knew  there was not only an oath of silence  but there were just replete with  informers I mean everybody want to kind  of stay personally in good with the FBI  and so yes there was a note of silence  and I think that oath really was drawn  in blood after the 16th Street Baptist  Church bombing we got very little help  there were informants yes and we had a  lot of informant information that  pointed to Cherry Blanton Chambliss and  others that may have been involved you  look at informant information that told  us that it was very planned that the  night before the bombing that all of the  clan leaders were very conspicuously  seen around Birmingham that night all  had their own alibis ready to go but at  when this bomb happened it clammed up it  shut up  and those people stayed silent whether  it was an oath or whether it was just  their own feelings of guilt and afraid  that they would to go to jail I don't  know but I do know this I know that  except for a couple of people that were  informants and that helped us in the  1960s we got virtually no help from  former Klansmen that we talked to that  I'm still convinced knew something about  this bomb  before or after it happened Doug Jones  served as the former US Attorney for  northern Alabama during the second half  of the Clinton administration we have  this tweet from one of our viewers Doug  Jones t says I thank you for your  commitment and bravery for prosecuting  the monsters behind the 1963 church  bombing you can share your thoughts the  hashtag at c-span history Michele is  joining us from Chester of Virginia go  ahead please thank you for having a more  kompis first of all my question to you  that you mentioned that you would like  for someone to speak regarding the  Klansmen you know to fill in the story I  wanted to know did the ex-wife or the  granddaughter are they able to fill in  the pieces because it would be nice to  have a complete a complete note of  history to fill in right no they didn't  fill in those kind of pieces they filled  in some interesting gaps for us because  of the admissions that were made the  ex-wife who came to Birmingham to  testify told us about a time when Bobby  Frank Cherry's car broke down just  outside the church and she came to pick  him up and he talked about where they  placed the bomb and there were other  times when he talked about the bomb but  they didn't fill in the gap she was not  married to him in 1963 so she didn't  know all of the details and he now I  don't think he ever shared the real  details about where the bomb was made  who was placed it and those kind of  things  the granddaughter and others mainly  talked about how a cherry after the fact  would brag about it she of course was  not born in 1963 either so it was the  bragging that took place afterwards that  I think helped seal the deal in the  Cherry case and and one other  interesting piece Steve that I wanted to  mention while we're talking about people  that talked about what sherry said in  1980 there was an individual who had  been from Birmingham that had moved to  Dallas Texas and he came forward when he  saw the media to tell us about a  conversation that he had with cherry in  1980 this is  twenty-something years ago almost 30  before the media showed that we had be  investigating the case and you told us  about a time when they were cleaning  some apartments in Dallas cherry had a  carpet cleaning business and they were  complaining people in the room were  complaining about the rise of the  Hispanic population in Dallas in 1980  and in 1980 even then Bobby Frank cherry  says but we just need to hand all the  mexicans like we did with and you can  imagine the racial slur that he used  talking about the black community in  birmingham alabama and so when we talk  about the lessons of history and and how  we need to remember the lessons of  history so that we don't make the same  mistakes i think that's probably the  most significant piece of testimony for  today's climate with all that we have  going on in our country with regard  immigration and the diversity that we  have tim is joining us Fort Wayne  Indiana welcome to the program Tim  thanks guys I had a couple quick  comments in a question I think the  church should be a historic site and  also a national holiday and my question  mr. Jones you mentioned the KKK Ku Klux  Klan are a bunch of idiots but what I  read in the past a lot of these the Ku  Klux Klan members were judges lawyers  doctors businessmen they wanted a bunch  of hayseeds and I think mr. cherry was a  fall guy and when was Mont Luther King's  home bombs not far from this date was at  least a few years before thank you yeah  his let me answer the question about the  Klan first and yes the Klan at the time  in the 30s 40s and 50s where there were  professionals there were judges there  were lawyers and remember one of our  great jurist Hugo black was at one point  a member of the Klan who later renounced  that but what we saw in Birmingham was  it not the Klan as a whole but there was  a group of what I call the more ignorant  Klansmen who only knew violence the  judges and the lawyers and people like  that I don't think we're in that  category but there were a group of folks  that all they could do and all they  couldn't respond  - were was with their hate was with  violence and those are the ones that I  think with the ignorant part of the Klan  that caused so much damage Martin Luther  King's home was bombed in Montgomery  Alabama about 90 miles south of here  in the 50s when he was a preacher at the  church down there that started the bus  boycott in Rosa Parks interestingly  after the Birmingham marches in the  spring of 1963 two weeks after those  marches were concluded dr. King's  brother the Reverend ad King who had a  church here in Birmingham at the time  his home was bombed just two weeks after  those marches we saw a lot more bombings  that summer here in Birmingham  Arthur Shores one of the great lawyers  his home was bombed not once but twice  that year so that that faction of the  Klan they were known as the Cahaba River  Bridge boys the violent faction of the  Klan were really kicking it into high  gear in the summer and fall of 1963 I  want to put one other event into  perspective because around the time of  the bombings 50 years ago dr. King was  sent to a Birmingham jail and he  received pressure from other ministers  in the Birmingham community and he wrote  a letter  can you explain well that was in the  spring of 1963 just so they started the  Birmingham marches in April of 1963  that's when dr. King was was arrested  interestingly Steve those Marxist people  forget sometimes those marches were  postponed on two different occasions  because people in Birmingham were  beginning to see change in November of  1962 they had voted to change the form  of government from a three-member  Commission to a mayor and city council  and and in just in February I believe it  was February of 63 there were elections  for the first city council of Birmingham  the first mayor of Birmingham in Bull  Connor was running for mayor and it was  thought that if the marches took place  it would might influence in a bad way of  that election when Bull Connor got in a  runoff with Albert Boutwell the marchers  were postponed again but as the marches  started after Bull Connor was defeated  dr. King decided he was going to go to  jail he and Reverend Abernathy went to  jail  and there had been a time that was  published a letter from some clergy that  was basically employing dr. King to just  wait to that Birmingham was beginning to  see changes to give the new city  government an opportunity to come and  make the changes necessary dr. King's  famous letter was essentially why we  can't wait it was a remarkable response  written on pieces scrap pieces of paper  newspaper  Paulette paper from the jail that was  snuck out by his lawyer Clarence Jones  and others that was then pieced together  a a lot of people think that that letter  got published immediately it didn't it  didn't really see very much light until  the summer of 1963 but today hands  stands I think as a testament as to one  of the great works of literature in  American history about the need for  action the need for peaceful action and  why you should not wait when you see  wrong so final question for you Doug  Jones what do you tell this generation  not old enough to remember what happened  fifty years ago and where we are today  as a country I tell them to be vigilant  to study their history to look and see  we we I don't think that the history of  the civil rights movement is taught at  schools like it should be black schools  white schools integrated schools it  doesn't matter people need to understand  the struggles because it was not only  dr. King it was the forces of good that  prevailed but you learn also from the  forces of evil because evil still exists  and today and as I said to the jury in  the chary case you know we had hope in  1963 the black community had a hope and  hope is a good thing and good things  never die and people like Bobby cherry  tried to kill that hope we still have  hope in this country today and our  children are the hope of our future and  our society is getting ever more diverse  in this country and it's not going to  change regardless of what we do with  immigration bills and things like that  and we need to make sure that we learn  those lessons and that we continue to  love respect and and and really  celebrate the diversity of this country  and embrace each other and that's the  lessons I think that is the lessons of  the past but as we're saying here in  that's the lessons for 50 years forward  not just backward Doug Jones responsible  for the prosecution of some of those  involved in the bombings that took place  50 years ago at the 16th Street Baptist  Church in Birmingham Alabama thanks very  much for being with us thank you Steve  enjoyed it and a reminder we are live  all day here on c-span threes American  history TV and you're looking at scenes  outside the 16th Street Baptist Church  which became a focal point of the civil  rights movement in the early and mid  1960s many attribute the bombing that  killed four little girls as one of the  moments that led to the Civil Rights Act  of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of  1965 a reminder at three o'clock Central  Time 4:00 o'clock here in the East live  coverage of a ceremony that will include  comments by US Attorn